;;; exc-calc-classes.el --- How Excel takes each function's arguments -*- lexical-binding: t -*-

;;; Commentary:

;; For each function, the class Excel gives what it returns and the
;; class of each of its arguments, as a workbook file records them in a
;; formula's tokens.  `exc-calc--token-classes' works out from them
;; where a formula that is not an array formula takes one value of a
;; range (implicit intersection) and where it takes all of them.
;;
;; Generated from the function tables of LibreOffice 4.4,
;; sc/source/filter/oox/formulabase.cxx (Mozilla Public License 2.0),
;; the last version that kept these classes.  Later versions of that
;; file reduce them all to one.
;;
;; Each entry is (NAME RETURN CLASSES . PAIRS).  RETURN is R for a
;; reference, V for a value and A for an array.  Each of CLASSES is the
;; type of an argument, R for a reference and V for a value, followed by
;; how it is converted: O keeps the argument's own class, V makes it a
;; value, A an array, R repeats the conversion of the function in an
;; argument that takes a value and X in one that takes a reference.  The
;; last class repeats for any further argument; with PAIRS the last two
;; repeat, in turn.

;;; Code:
